findings.body:
The removed `BLOCKED` guard (`__proto__`, `constructor`, `prototype`) was the only protection against prototype pollution in this recursive merge.

**Failure mode:** A patch such as `{"__proto__": {"polluted": true}}` or `{"constructor": {"prototype": {"polluted": true}}}` — both valid `JSON.parse` output — reaches `Object.entries`, then `(base as any)[k] ??= {}` / assignment. For `__proto__`, the read returns `Object.prototype` (truthy), so the recursive call merges attacker keys straight onto `Object.prototype`. The `constructor.prototype` path does the same. Verified: after merge, `{}.polluted === true`.

**Fix:** Restore the blocklist (or equivalent `Object.hasOwn` + skip, or merge into `Object.create(null)` targets only). Do not rely on “JSON.parse is safe” — it preserves these keys as own properties.

```suggestion
const BLOCKED = new Set(["__proto__", "constructor", "prototype"]);

export function deepMerge<T>(base: T, patch: Record<string, unknown>): T {
  for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(patch)) {
    if (BLOCKED.has(k)) continue;
```
